  "hints": [
    "For a fixed bar i that is the limiting (shortest) bar, how wide can the rectangle be? You need the nearest strictly lower bar on each side.",
    "Nearest-smaller-on-each-side is exactly what a monotonic stack gives you in amortised O(1) per bar. Do you need two passes or can one do both?",
    "Keep a stack of bars with increasing heights. When a bar of height h arrives, pop every bar taller or equal to h: for a popped bar the right limit is i and the left limit is the new stack top. Use a sentinel height 0 at the end and 64-bit area."
  ],
  "editorial": "Every maximal rectangle is limited by some bar: its height equals that bar's height and it extends left and right until a strictly lower bar appears. So for each bar we need the nearest lower bar on each side.\n\nProcess bars left to right with a stack whose heights are strictly increasing. When bar `i` arrives, pop all bars with height `>= h[i]`. For a popped bar of height `H`, the right boundary is `i` (the first bar that is not taller) and the left boundary is the bar now at the top of the stack, which is strictly lower. The rectangle has width `i - (top + 1)` (or `i` if the stack is empty) and area `H * width`. A sentinel `0` at the end flushes everything.\n\nEqual-height bars are handled because the earlier equal bar is popped with a too-short width, but the later equal bar gets the full width. Each bar is pushed/popped once, `O(n)`. Areas reach `n · 10^9`, so use 64-bit integers.",
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(n)"
  },
